course: dictionary
lesson: 8026
title: Reform: change for the better
level: primary-4
stars: 0
explain: To reform something means to change it so that it gets better. A school can reform its rules, and a government can reform a law. Reform can be a verb (what you do) or a noun (the change itself).
explain: Use the words around reform to work out its meaning. In «She reformed tax collection», the word tax tells you it is about money, and the idea of changing something to work better tells you the meaning. Ask yourself: does the change make things better? If yes, you have found the meaning.
explain: Reform can also describe a person who has changed for the better. A reformed character is someone who used to be bad but now behaves well. Look at the word before reform: a reformed thief has stopped stealing.
explain: With a hyphen, re-form means to form again. «The line re-formed after the bell» means the children made a line again. When you see the hyphen, read the rest of the sentence to see which meaning fits.
fact: Reform means to change something to make it better.
fact: «She reformed tax collection» means she changed how taxes were gathered so that it worked better.
fact: A reform, as a noun, is a change that improves something, such as school reforms.
fact: A reformed person has changed their behaviour and become better, like a reformed thief.
fact: Re-form, with a hyphen, means to form again, as in «the line re-formed».
exercise: What does reform mean? | To stop a game in the middle | To break something into small pieces | *To change something to make it better | To sing very loudly
exercise: «She reformed tax collection.» What does reformed mean here? | Lost in the post | Forgot all about it | Gave it away for free | *Changed to make it work better
exercise: «The villain was a reformed character by the end.» What does reformed show about him? | *He had changed and become a better person | He was tired and hungry | He had moved to a new house | He was still as bad as ever
test: What does the noun reform mean? | *A change that improves something | A loud noise | A cup for tea | A shadow on the wall
test: Nadia says reform and re-form mean the same. What is the difference? | They mean exactly the same thing | Reform is a food, and re-form is a drink | Reform is always a person, never a thing | *Reform means to change for the better, and re-form means to form again
exam: Ali reads «She reformed tax collection» and says reform means to ruin something. What is the best answer? | Ali is right, because ruin and reform mean the same | Reform means to eat something quickly | *Ali is wrong, because reform means to change something to make it better | Reform is a colour, so it cannot be a change
